 "beats": [
  {
   "id": "o1",
   "text": "Hollywood made her a star. Then it filmed her songs so that theaters in the South could cut her out of the movie.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "p1946",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.05
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.62,
     0.4
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "hook": "Hollywood made her a star. *Then filmed her so the South could cut her out.*"
   },
   "captions": false
  },
  {
   "id": "o2",
   "text": "She was the first Black woman in decades to sign a long-term contract with a major Hollywood studio.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "sing1",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "o3",
   "text": "She would not play maids. So the studio barely let her act at all.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "sing3",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "o3b",
   "text": "This is the story of a woman Hollywood wanted to show off, and hide, at the same time. And of how she refused to stay hidden.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "p1955",
    "caption": "Lena Horne"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "o4",
   "text": "Her name was Lena Horne. This is her story.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Lena Horne",
    "sub": "singer, actor, activist"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e1",
   "text": "Lena Mary Calhoun Horne was born on June 30th, 1917, in Brooklyn, New York.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "p1947",
    "caption": "Lena Horne"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "pin": {
     "label": "Brooklyn, New York"
    }
   },
   "section": "early"
  },
  {
   "id": "e2",
   "text": "Her family belonged to the educated Black middle class of New York. Relatives held posts in the NAACP and the Urban League.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Brooklyn",
    "sub": "the Black middle class"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e3",
   "text": "Her father left when she was three. Her mother was an actress who toured with a Black theater company.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "p1941",
    "caption": "Lena Horne, 1941"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e4",
   "text": "So Lena was raised mostly by her grandparents, Cora and Edwin Horne, with long stretches traveling with her mother, including years in Georgia.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Georgia",
    "sub": "part of her childhood"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "pin": {
     "label": "Fort Valley, Georgia"
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e5",
   "text": "At sixteen, she left school.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "govprop",
    "caption": "Lena Horne"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e5b",
   "text": "By then she had already learned what it meant to be a Black girl moving between North and South, and between her mother's world and her grandmother's.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "govprop",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.15,
     1.25
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.42
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "h1",
   "text": "In 1933 she joined the chorus line at the Cotton Club in Harlem.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "stage",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1933"
   },
   "section": "harlem"
  },
  {
   "id": "h2",
   "text": "The performers were Black. The audience was white only.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "The Cotton Club",
    "sub": "Black performers, white audience"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "h3",
   "text": "Then she sang with Noble Sissle's orchestra, and made her first records.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "stage2",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "h4",
   "text": "In 1937 she married Louis Jones. Their daughter Gail was born that December.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "p1946",
    "caption": "Lena Horne, 1946"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1937"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "h5",
   "text": "In 1938, she starred in her first feature film, The Duke Is Tops, made for Black audiences.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "cooper",
    "focus": [
     0.62,
     0.32
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "lowerThird": {
     "name": "The Duke Is Tops",
     "sub": "1938"
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "h6",
   "text": "It was a low-budget picture. She later said she was never paid for it.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "cooper2",
    "focus": [
     0.62,
     0.32
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "h7",
   "text": "Years later, when she was famous, it was sold again under a new name, with her on top of the poster.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "bronze",
    "caption": "The Bronze Venus, the same film re-released"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "h7b",
   "text": "But in 1938, she was still a band singer, earning very little.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "sing5",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.06,
     1.12
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "b1",
   "text": "In 1940 she joined the white bandleader Charlie Barnet on tour.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "sing2",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1940"
   },
   "section": "band"
  },
  {
   "id": "b2",
   "text": "She was the only Black member of the band. On the road, she faced discrimination, and she quit.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "The only Black member",
    "sub": "Charlie Barnet's band"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "b3",
   "text": "In 1941 she sang at Cafe Society in Greenwich Village, New York's first integrated nightclub.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "sing4",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1941"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "b4",
   "text": "There she worked with Paul Robeson, the singer and activist.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "p1941",
    "caption": "Lena Horne, 1941"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m1",
   "text": "Then Hollywood came calling. In 1942, MGM signed her to a seven-year contract.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "vv1941",
    "caption": "Lena Horne, photographed by Carl Van Vechten, 1941"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1942"
   },
   "section": "mgm"
  },
  {
   "id": "m2",
   "text": "It was the first long-term contract a major studio had given a Black woman in decades.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Seven years",
    "sub": "MGM, 1942"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m3",
   "text": "And it said she would not be cast as a maid or as comic relief.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "fuel",
    "caption": "Lena Horne, World War II poster"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m3b",
   "text": "Her first film for MGM was Panama Hattie, in 1942.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Panama Hattie",
    "sub": "MGM, 1942"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m4",
   "text": "The NAACP's leader, Walter White, told her she was a test case for how Hollywood would treat Black women.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "A test case",
    "sub": "Walter White, NAACP"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m5",
   "text": "But MGM did not know what to do with her.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "sing5",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m6",
   "text": "In most of her films she simply sang, alone, in a scene of her own, with no white star beside her.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "clouds",
    "caption": "Lena Horne in Till the Clouds Roll By, 1946"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m6b",
   "text": "In Ziegfeld Follies and in Words and Music, it was the same: a song, a spotlight, and a scene that could be lifted out of the film without breaking the story.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "p1946",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.15,
     1.25
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.42
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m7",
   "text": "That was on purpose. Those scenes could be cut out easily, for theaters in the South that would not show Black performers.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Made to be cut",
    "sub": "for theaters in the South"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m8",
   "text": "In some cities, including Memphis, they were.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "sailor",
    "caption": "Lena Horne in Two Girls and a Sailor, 1944"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m8b",
   "text": "She was one of the most photographed women in Hollywood. And in much of the country, audiences never saw her on screen at all.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "gottlieb",
    "caption": "Lena Horne, 1940s"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "s1",
   "text": "In 1943 she got two real roles, both in films with all-Black casts.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "cabin",
    "caption": "Cabin in the Sky, 1943"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1943"
   },
   "section": "stormy"
  },
  {
   "id": "s2",
   "text": "In Cabin in the Sky, she played the temptress Georgia Brown.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "cabin",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "s3",
   "text": "Then MGM lent her to another studio for Stormy Weather.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "stormy",
    "caption": "Stormy Weather, 1943"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "s4",
   "text": "She starred beside the great tap dancer Bill Robinson.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "stormylc",
    "caption": "Lena Horne and Bill Robinson, Stormy Weather"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "s5",
   "text": "And the title song, Stormy Weather, became her song for the rest of her life.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "stormy3",
    "caption": "Bill Robinson, Lena Horne and Cab Calloway"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "w1",
   "text": "During World War II, she was a favorite of Black soldiers. Her picture hung in barracks across the country.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "tuskegee",
    "caption": "Lena Horne with Tuskegee airmen, 1945"
   },
   "section": "war"
  },
  {
   "id": "w2",
   "text": "She helped launch a Liberty ship named for George Washington Carver.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "carver",
    "caption": "Launching the SS George Washington Carver, 1943"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "w3",
   "text": "And she sang for the troops. At one Army camp, she came on stage and saw the seating.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bwd3",
    "focus": [
     0.55,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "w4",
   "text": "German prisoners of war sat in the front rows. The Black American soldiers sat behind them.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "In the front rows",
    "sub": "German prisoners of war"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "w5",
   "text": "She walked off the stage, past the prisoners, and sang to the Black soldiers at the back.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bwd2",
    "focus": [
     0.55,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "w6",
   "text": "She also complained to the NAACP. She refused to perform for segregated audiences.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "govprop",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.42
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "w7",
   "text": "She also worked with Eleanor Roosevelt to push for laws against lynching.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Against lynching",
    "sub": "with Eleanor Roosevelt"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "l1",
   "text": "After the war, she fell in love with Lennie Hayton, MGM's white musical director.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "hayton",
    "caption": "Lena Horne and Lennie Hayton"
   },
   "section": "love"
  },
  {
   "id": "l2",
   "text": "In California, a marriage between them was against the law.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Against the law",
    "sub": "California, 1940s"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "l3",
   "text": "So in December 1947 they married in Paris. They stayed together until his death.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "gottlieb",
    "caption": "Lena Horne, about 1947"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "December 1947",
    "pin": {
     "label": "Paris"
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "l4",
   "text": "Then came the part she wanted most. MGM was remaking Show Boat, with a role she had already played on screen: Julie, a woman of mixed race.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "clouds",
    "caption": "Lena Horne as Julie, Till the Clouds Roll By, 1946"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "l5",
   "text": "The role went to Ava Gardner, a white actress.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Julie",
    "sub": "Show Boat, 1951"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "l6",
   "text": "By 1950 she had left MGM. And in the early 1950s, she was blacklisted for her political ties, including her friendship with Paul Robeson.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "p1955",
    "caption": "Lena Horne, 1955"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1950s"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "l6b",
   "text": "For a few years, the television and radio work dried up. She was cleared by the mid-1950s, and she later spoke out against communism.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "p1947",
    "caption": "Lena Horne"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "n1",
   "text": "Hollywood had shut its doors. So she went back to where she started: the nightclub stage.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bwd1",
    "focus": [
     0.55,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   },
   "section": "clubs"
  },
  {
   "id": "n2",
   "text": "She signed with RCA Victor and became one of the top nightclub stars in America.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Nightclub star",
    "sub": "RCA Victor, 1956"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "n3",
   "text": "In 1957 she starred on Broadway in the musical Jamaica.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "p1955",
    "caption": "Lena Horne"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1957"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "n4",
   "text": "The woman Hollywood had kept in separate scenes was now the star of the whole show.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bwd1",
    "focus": [
     0.55,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.06,
     1.12
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "r1",
   "text": "In the 1960s, she put her fame to work for civil rights.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "p1961",
    "caption": "Lena Horne, 1961"
   },
   "section": "rights"
  },
  {
   "id": "r2",
   "text": "In 1963 she sang at a civil rights rally in Jackson, Mississippi, where Medgar Evers spoke. Days later, he was murdered.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Jackson, Mississippi",
    "sub": "1963"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "r3",
   "text": "That August, she joined the March on Washington.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "march",
    "caption": "Lena Horne at the March on Washington, 1963"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "28 August 1963"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "r4",
   "text": "Three months later, she was at the White House with President Kennedy, two days before he was killed.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "jfk",
    "caption": "At the White House, 20 November 1963"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "r5",
   "text": "She was no longer just a singer who happened to be Black. She was using her name to ask the country to change.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "p1961",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.42
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "x1",
   "text": "Then came the hardest years of her life.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "gottlieb",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.45
    ]
   },
   "section": "losses"
  },
  {
   "id": "x2",
   "text": "In 1970 her father died, and so did her son, Teddy.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "1970",
    "sub": "her father, her son"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "x3",
   "text": "In 1971, Lennie Hayton died too.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "1971",
    "sub": "Lennie Hayton"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "x4",
   "text": "She stopped performing for a time.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "p1947",
    "caption": "Lena Horne, 1947"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "x5",
   "text": "In 1978 she came back to the screen, playing Glinda the Good Witch in the film The Wiz.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "The Wiz",
    "sub": "Glinda, 1978"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c1",
   "text": "Then, in May 1981, at sixty-three, she opened a one-woman show on Broadway: Lena Horne, The Lady and Her Music.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "sing2",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.06,
     1.12
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "May 1981"
   },
   "section": "broadway"
  },
  {
   "id": "c2",
   "text": "It ran for 333 performances.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "333 performances",
    "sub": "Broadway, 1981 to 1982"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c3",
   "text": "It closed on her sixty-fifth birthday. It won her a special Tony Award, and the album won two Grammy Awards.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "sing3",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.06,
     1.12
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c4",
   "text": "In 1984 she received the Kennedy Center Honors.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Kennedy Center Honors",
    "sub": "1984"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c5",
   "text": "In 2022, a Broadway theater was renamed the Lena Horne Theatre, the first Broadway house named for a Black woman.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "The Lena Horne Theatre",
    "sub": "Broadway, 2022"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d1",
   "text": "Lena Horne died on May 9th, 2010, in New York. She was 92.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "vv1941",
    "caption": "Lena Horne, 1917-2010"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "9 May 2010"
   },
   "section": "end"
  },
  {
   "id": "d2",
   "text": "She had spent her life being told where she could sing, where she could sit, and whom she could marry.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "hayton",
    "caption": "Lena Horne and Lennie Hayton"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "z1",
   "text": "Hollywood filmed her so she could be cut out. She made sure she never was.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "sing1",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.06,
     1.12
    ]
   },
   "section": "close"
  },
  {
   "id": "z2",
   "text": "That was Lena Horne. Subscribe for more.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "endcard"
   },
   "captions": false
  }
 ],
